-- **Test classes that start a tool under a hard time limit share one xunit collection (experiment; card 368 retro, Decider D247).** While this entry is on trial: an acceptance test class that starts the bench tools or another external process under a hard time limit (for example the 20 second limit of the bench timeout test) carries `[Collection("Bench processes")]`, so that two such classes never overlap in one full run; the independent gate reviewer checks every new class of that kind by hand and records it in its review until an automated guard exists; the bug filed with this pick makes the classes CSharpBaselineAcceptance and PrivateHoldoutResults, which still overlap, join the collection AND adds that guard, written red first: a test that fails when a test class that starts the bench tools or another external process lacks the collection attribute, so that CI, not a reviewer, catches a missed class.
-  - Hypothesis: a time-limited test that never competes with another process-starting class stops failing under load. Metric: sign-off attempts per card (card 368: 2, the first failed on the timing test in two of four full runs), target 1, and full-suite timing failures, target 0. Counter-metric: minutes per full suite (card 368: about 10) and the seconds the serial collection adds. Trial: the next 3 cards that add or touch a test class that starts the bench tools or another external process, counted from Decider D247. Owner: the facilitator. Review at the third card's retro: keep, change or remove.
+- **A test that asserts a wall-clock limit runs alone in an isolated collection (experiment; card 368 retro, Decider D247; rewritten by the card 417 retro, Decider D254).** While this entry is on trial: an acceptance test class that asserts a wall-clock limit (a stopwatch's `Elapsed`, total seconds or total milliseconds compared with a limit, or a cancellation token created with a time limit and used to fail a test) carries `[Collection("Isolated timing")]`; one `[CollectionDefinition("Isolated timing", DisableParallelization = true)]` exists in the test project, so xunit runs that collection after every parallel collection has finished, with nothing else running; the guard test `WallClockTestsRunIsolated` (card 417) fails the build when a class that asserts a wall-clock limit does not carry the collection, and its test of the definition fails when the definition is missing or weakened, so the independent gate reviewer no longer checks this by hand; the earlier remedy of D247 (the shared `[Collection("Bench processes")]` for classes that start the bench tools) is dropped as a remedy for a time limit: it did not stop the timing test failing under load (card 418: twice), because the load came from other parallel classes; the classes that already carry it may keep it as a harmless grouping.
+  - Hypothesis: a test that asserts a wall-clock limit and runs alone, after the parallel collections, stops failing under load. Metric: full-run timing failures (cards 368, 369 and 418: 4 in 8 full runs; card 417: 0 in 4), target 0. Counter-metric: acceptance minutes against the fixed 15 minute ceiling (card 417: 10.1 to 10.6) and benign names the guard raises (target 0). Trial: the next 3 cards that add or touch a test class that asserts a wall-clock limit or change the isolated collection or its guard, counted from Decider D254. Owner: the facilitator. Review at the third such card's retro: keep, change or remove.
